## Analysis

The current program is already correct and minimal in source terms. The score depends only on the number of SSA instructions executed, so the lever is the per-word cost of the popcount, not the source length.

`bits.OnesCount64` is a call into the standard library. In an SSA interpreter like giri, that adds call overhead: argument setup, frame handling, and the return. The callee body is also a separate set of instructions per word. Inlining the SWAR (SIMD-within-a-register) popcount directly in the loop removes the call and keeps the work to a fixed sequence of arithmetic ops. The multiply-and-shift at the end sums the eight byte counts in one operation.

Since the only remaining use of `math/bits` would be gone, the import must be removed too, or the file won't compile.

The loop, the accumulator, and the signature stay as they are. The result is the same for every uint64 input, so correctness is preserved, and `xs` is only read, so it is left unchanged.

I can't measure the exact cost delta here, so this is a hypothesis. If giri already inlines `OnesCount64` as a single intrinsic, the gain will be small or none. The SWAR version is the more likely win because it avoids the call.

